        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "89aad4ff-37e9-5524-87b5-7102c22f2f2e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Malcolm Arnold, Queen Elizabeth II, Humphrey Searle, Oliver Messel\n- Organizations: Sadler's Wells Ballet, Royal Opera House Covent Garden\n- Works: Homage to the Queen, Op. 42\n- Events: 1953 coronation, first performance\n\nFacts:\n- In 1953, Malcolm Arnold wrote Homage to the Queen, Op. 42 as the official coronation ballet.\n- Sadler's Wells Ballet commissioned Homage to the Queen, Op. 42 in honour of Queen Elizabeth II.\n- Humphrey Searle, the musical adviser to Sadler's Wells Ballet, recommended Malcolm Arnold for the commission.\n- Homage to the Queen, Op. 42 was first performed by Sadler's Wells Ballet on Coronation night, 2 June 1953, at the Royal Opera House Covent Garden.\n- Oliver Messel created the original scenery and costumes for Homage to the Queen, Op. 42.\n- Oliver Hilary Sambourne Messel lived from 13 January 1904 to 13 July 1978.\n- Oliver Messel was an English artist and one of the foremost stage designers of the 20th century.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "f845cfc8-fa45-5f0f-8673-4a5e32fe2877",
        "text": "Page: Homage to the Queen. Sentence: Homage to the Queen, Op. 42, by Malcolm Arnold was written as the official coronation ballet in 1953, commissioned by the Sadler's Wells Ballet in honour of Queen Elizabeth II, the ballet company's musical adviser Humphrey Searle having recommended Arnold for the job.\nPage: Homage to the Queen. Sentence: It was first performed by the Sadler's Wells Ballet on Coronation night 2 June 1953 at the Royal Opera House Covent Garden, with original scenery and costumes by Oliver Messel.\nPage: Oliver Messel. Sentence: Oliver Hilary Sambourne Messel (13 January 1904 – 13 July 1978) was an English artist and one of the foremost stage designers of the 20th century.",
